{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every natural number n, every probability measure μ on Ω = {false,true}^{Fin(n+1)}, and every x,y ∈ Ω, if μ({x}) > 0, then the singleton transition mass of the defined random-scan kernel is Pμ(x,{y}) = (n+1)⁻¹ ∑_{i∈Fin(n+1)} [if y_j = x_j for every j ≠ i, then μ({z∈Ω : r_i(z)=r_i(x)})⁻¹ μ({y}), else 0], where r_i deletes coordinate i. The kernel Pμ is the uniform mixture of the coordinate-update kernels constructed using the library-selected regular conditional distributions. Arithmetic in the displayed Lean equality takes place in the extended nonnegative reals.",
